WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.812
Lesson 3 is where magnetism stops being a static map topic and turns into a motion topic.

2
00:00:06.812 --> 00:00:13.623
A current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field feels a force.

3
00:00:13.623 --> 00:00:20.435
But that force is not along the field lines and it is not automatically along the current either.

4
00:00:20.435 --> 00:00:27.247
It is sideways to both.

5
00:00:27.247 --> 00:00:34.058
That is why the lesson needs careful direction discipline.

6
00:00:34.058 --> 00:00:40.870
Once the learner keeps field direction, current direction, and force direction separate, the force formula becomes meaningful instead of symbolic clutter.

7
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:47.682
The worked example then becomes a clean two-part task: find the magnitude using the factors in the formula, and decide the direction by changing one direction at a time.

8
00:00:47.682 --> 00:00:54.493
Keep this takeaway in view: The motor effect becomes manageable once three directions are kept separate before any rule is applied.

9
00:00:54.493 --> 00:01:01.305
Field means sets one direction.

10
00:01:01.305 --> 00:01:08.117
The magnetic field direction is one of the ingredients, but it is not the final force direction.

11
00:01:08.117 --> 00:01:14.928
Current means sets another direction.

12
00:01:14.928 --> 00:01:21.740
The current direction must stay separate so the direction check remains trustworthy.

13
00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.683
This diagram matters because it gives the force a geometry.

14
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:33.626
The conductor sits in a magnetic field, and current flows through it.

15
00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:39.569
The resulting force is not a vague consequence of being near a magnet.

16
00:01:39.569 --> 00:01:45.512
It depends on the crossing between current and field.

17
00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:51.455
The force is strongest when that crossing is at ninety degrees, and the force direction flips if either the current or the field flips.

18
00:01:51.455 --> 00:01:57.398
That means the picture is doing more than illustrating a fact.

19
00:01:57.398 --> 00:02:03.341
It is training the learner to separate the input directions from the output direction.

20
00:02:03.341 --> 00:02:09.284
Keep this takeaway in view: The diagram turns the motor effect into a direction structure rather than a memorized sentence.

21
00:02:09.284 --> 00:02:15.227
Crossing matters: The effect depends on how the current-carrying section sits relative to the field.

22
00:02:15.227 --> 00:02:21.170
Sideways result: The force is perpendicular to the field and to the current.

23
00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:28.062
An analogy that helps here is a skater moving through a corridor where powerful air jets cross the route from the side.

24
00:02:28.062 --> 00:02:34.954
The skater's own motion direction is one ingredient, and the direction of the air jets is another.

25
00:02:34.954 --> 00:02:41.846
The effect the skater feels is a sideways shove that depends on both.

26
00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:48.738
The analogy is not the same physics, but it helps students feel why the result should not be read as forward along the corridor.

27
00:02:48.738 --> 00:02:55.630
In the motor effect, the conductor and field create a sideways response, and that is the idea the lesson most needs to protect.

28
00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:02.522
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy is useful because it makes perpendicular force feel natural before the vector rule appears.

29
00:03:02.522 --> 00:03:09.414
Skater path means like current direction.

30
00:03:09.414 --> 00:03:16.306
One direction belongs to the moving route itself.

31
00:03:16.306 --> 00:03:23.198
Air-jet direction means like field direction.

32
00:03:23.198 --> 00:03:30.090
A second direction crosses the route and changes the outcome.

33
00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:36.108
The key relation here is F equals B I L sine theta.

34
00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:42.125
Each symbol belongs to the physical story.

35
00:03:42.125 --> 00:03:48.143
B is the field strength, I is the current, L is the conductor length in the field, and theta is the angle between the conductor current direction and the field.

36
00:03:48.143 --> 00:03:54.161
The sine factor matters because the force is largest at ninety degrees and falls to zero when the conductor is parallel to the field.

37
00:03:54.161 --> 00:04:00.178
So the formula does not replace the concept.

38
00:04:00.178 --> 00:04:06.196
It sharpens it.

39
00:04:06.196 --> 00:04:12.214
The learner still has to decide what the angle is and still has to keep the direction check separate from the magnitude calculation.

40
00:04:12.214 --> 00:04:18.232
Keep this takeaway in view: The force formula is safest when angle and direction are kept in separate reasoning lanes.

41
00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:24.249
Magnitude job: The formula tells how big the force is when the factor values and angle are known.

42
00:04:24.249 --> 00:04:30.267
Direction job: The force direction still needs a left-hand-rule or reversal-style direction check.

43
00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:36.285
F = B I L sin(theta).

44
00:04:36.285 --> 00:04:42.302
maximum force when theta = 90 deg.

45
00:04:42.302 --> 00:04:48.320
Use the formula for magnitude, then handle the force direction as a separate reasoning step.

46
00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.861
A strong motor-effect routine has four moves.

47
00:04:53.861 --> 00:04:59.402
First place field direction, current direction, and force direction into separate mental slots.

48
00:04:59.402 --> 00:05:04.942
Second identify the angle between current and field.

49
00:05:04.942 --> 00:05:10.483
Third use the formula to calculate the force magnitude.

50
00:05:10.483 --> 00:05:16.024
Fourth test what happens when current or field reverses so the direction answer is stated cleanly.

51
00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:21.565
This routine matters because students who rush straight to the equation often calculate a number correctly but explain the direction badly.

52
00:05:21.565 --> 00:05:27.106
The routine prevents that split.

53
00:05:27.106 --> 00:05:32.647
Keep this takeaway in view: Separate slots, then angle, then magnitude, then direction is the safest routine for Lesson 3.

54
00:05:32.647 --> 00:05:38.188
Step 1 means separate the slots.

55
00:05:38.188 --> 00:05:43.728
Field, current, and force directions should never share one vague arrow.

56
00:05:43.728 --> 00:05:49.269
Step 2 means read the angle.

57
00:05:49.269 --> 00:05:54.810
The angle belongs between current direction and field direction.

58
00:05:54.810 --> 00:06:00.603
The left-hand rule is useful, but only after the ingredients are labeled properly.

59
00:06:00.603 --> 00:06:06.396
If the learner has not already decided which arrow is the field and which arrow is the current, the hand rule cannot rescue the answer.

60
00:06:06.396 --> 00:06:12.189
It will only formalize a confusion that already exists.

61
00:06:12.189 --> 00:06:17.982
That is why the lesson keeps returning to the three-direction slots.

62
00:06:17.982 --> 00:06:23.775
The rule is a confirmation tool, not a substitute for reading the diagram carefully.

63
00:06:23.775 --> 00:06:29.568
Keep this takeaway in view: The left-hand rule is strongest as a confirmation step, not as the first step.

64
00:06:29.568 --> 00:06:35.361
Use the rule after means labeling the inputs.

65
00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:41.154
Field and current must be identified correctly before the rule can produce a reliable force direction.

66
00:06:41.154 --> 00:06:46.947
Main trap means gesture without labels.

67
00:06:46.947 --> 00:06:52.740
A correct hand shape cannot fix a wrongly identified field or current arrow.

68
00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:59.267
Many textbook questions choose the right-angle case because it makes the arithmetic simpler, but the full lesson should still explain why the angle matters.

69
00:06:59.267 --> 00:07:05.795
When the conductor is parallel to the field, the magnetic effect that causes the sideways force disappears, so the force drops to zero.

70
00:07:05.795 --> 00:07:12.322
As the angle opens toward ninety degrees, the force grows toward its maximum.

71
00:07:12.322 --> 00:07:18.849
That means the sine term is not decoration.

72
00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:25.376
It captures how strongly the conductor is crossing the field.

73
00:07:25.376 --> 00:07:31.904
Giving this idea its own scene makes the lesson richer and prepares students for less standard questions.

74
00:07:31.904 --> 00:07:38.431
Keep this takeaway in view: The sine factor matters because the motor effect depends on crossing geometry, not just on being near a field.

75
00:07:38.431 --> 00:07:44.958
Parallel case means no force.

76
00:07:44.958 --> 00:07:51.485
When current direction is parallel to the field, the crossing effect vanishes.

77
00:07:51.485 --> 00:07:58.013
Right-angle case means maximum force.

78
00:07:58.013 --> 00:08:04.540
At ninety degrees the conductor crosses the field most strongly.

79
00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.943
Ask four checks.

80
00:08:07.943 --> 00:08:11.347
Is the force on the wire along the field?

81
00:08:11.347 --> 00:08:14.750
No.

82
00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:18.153
Is it along the current?

83
00:08:18.153 --> 00:08:21.557
No.

84
00:08:21.557 --> 00:08:24.960
Is it largest when the wire is at ninety degrees to the field?

85
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.363
Yes.

86
00:08:28.363 --> 00:08:31.767
And if the current reverses while the field stays fixed, does the force reverse?

87
00:08:31.767 --> 00:08:35.170
Yes.

88
00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:38.573
Those checks prove that the learner now owns both the geometry and the reversal logic of the motor effect.

89
00:08:38.573 --> 00:08:41.977
Keep this takeaway in view: The quick check confirms that magnitude and direction are now being handled cleanly.

90
00:08:41.977 --> 00:08:45.380
Geometry check means sideways force.

91
00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:48.783
The force is perpendicular to both field and current.

92
00:08:48.783 --> 00:08:52.187
Angle check means largest at ninety degrees.

93
00:08:52.187 --> 00:08:55.590
The conductor feels the maximum force when it crosses the field most strongly.

94
00:08:55.590 --> 00:09:02.134
The worked example that follows has two jobs.

95
00:09:02.134 --> 00:09:08.677
First identify the angle and use B I L sine theta to find the force magnitude.

96
00:09:08.677 --> 00:09:15.221
Second decide what happens to the force direction if the current reverses.

97
00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:21.765
That means you should enter the example with the same routine already prepared: separate field, current, and force directions; calculate the size; then say how reversal changes the direction.

98
00:09:21.765 --> 00:09:28.308
Once that order is in place, the question reads like a short checklist instead of a trap.

99
00:09:28.308 --> 00:09:34.852
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is easiest when force size and force direction are handled as two linked but separate tasks.

100
00:09:34.852 --> 00:09:41.395
First move: Read the angle between current direction and field.

101
00:09:41.395 --> 00:09:47.939
Second move: Calculate the force magnitude using the motor-effect formula.

102
00:09:47.939 --> 00:09:54.483
label field, current, and force separately.

103
00:09:54.483 --> 00:10:01.026
F = B I L sin(theta).

104
00:10:01.026 --> 00:10:07.570
Magnitude and direction are related, but they should be answered in two clean stages.
